%I A399717 #24 Sep 26 2026 21:26:31
%S A399717 1,0,1,2,3,5,8,11,14,25,36,47,58,69,80,91,102,113,124,135,146,157,168,
%T A399717 179,190,201,212,223,234,245,256,267,278,289,300,311,322,333,344,355,
%U A399717 699,1054,1409,1764,2119,2474,2829,3184,3539,3894,4249,4604,4959,5314
%N A399717 Numerators of "Farey fraction" approximations to Pi/2.
%C A399717 Given a real number x >= 1 (here x = Pi/2), start with 1/0 and 0/1 and construct the sequence of fractions f_n = r_n/s_n such that:
%C A399717 f_{n+1} = (r_k + r_n)/(s_k + s_n) where k is the greatest integer < n such that f_k <= x <= f_n. Sequence gives values r_n.
%C A399717 Write a 0 if f_n <= x and a 1 if f_n > x. This gives (for x = Pi/2) the sequence 1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1, 0 (31 times), 1, 0 (145 times), ... Ignore the initial 1, which is the term 1/0 and is the same for every x >= 1. Look at the run lengths of the remaining sequence, which are in this case L_1 = 2, L_2 = 1, L_3 = 1, L_4 = 3, L_5 = 31, L_6 = 1, L_7 = 145, etc., the continued fraction of Pi/2 (A053300). Christoffel showed that x has the continued fraction representation (L_1 - 1) + 1/(L_2 + 1/(L_3 + 1/(L_4 + ...))).
%D A399717 Edward J. Barbeau, Pell's Equation, New York: Springer-Verlag, 2003, p. 56, Exercise 1.4.
%D A399717 C. Brezinski, History of Continued Fractions and Padé Approximants, Springer-Verlag, 1991; pp. 151-152.
%D A399717 E. B. Christoffel, Observatio arithmetica, Ann. Math. Pura Appl., (II) 6 (1875), 148-153.
%H A399717 Jwalin Bhatt, <a href="/A399717/b399717.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%F A399717 lim_{n->oo} a(n) / A399718(n) = Pi/2.
%e A399717 The fractions are 1/0, 0/1, 1/1, 2/1, 3/2, 5/3, 8/5, 11/7, 14/9, 25/16, 36/23, 47/30, ...
%t A399717 f[x_, n_] := (m = Floor[x]; f0 = {m, m + 1/2, m + 1};
%t A399717   r = ({a___, b_, c_, d___} /; b < x < c) :>
%t A399717       {b, (Numerator[b] + Numerator[c])/(Denominator[b] + Denominator[c]), c};
%t A399717   Join[{m, m + 1}, NestList[# /. r &, f0, n - 3][[All, 2]]]);
%t A399717 Join[{1, 0}, Numerator[f[Pi/2, 52]]]
%o A399717 (Python)
%o A399717 from sympy import Rational, pi
%o A399717 lo, hi = (0, 1), (1, 0)
%o A399717 A399717 = [1, 0]
%o A399717 for _ in range(52):
%o A399717     m = lo[0] + hi[0], lo[1] + hi[1]
%o A399717     A399717.append(m[0])
%o A399717     if Rational(m[0], m[1]) <= pi/2:
%o A399717         lo = m
%o A399717     else:
%o A399717         hi = m
%o A399717 print(A399717)
%Y A399717 Cf. A019669, A053300, A063674, A097545, A399718 (denominators).
%K A399717 nonn,frac,new
%O A399717 0,4
%A A399717 _Jwalin Bhatt_, Sep 09 2026
